A growable sequence of values of one type.

Use alloc and core.

A public record List of T has a data (a pointer to T), a len (an unsigned number), and a cap (an unsigned number).

Publicly, to create an empty list for T, named list_new, giving List of T.
Let created be List of T.
Give back created.

Publicly, to push for T val (a T) to items (a pointer to List of T), named list_push.
If items's len equals items's cap.
Let new cap be items's cap times 2.
If items's cap equals 0, make new cap 8 as an unsigned number.
If new cap is less than 16, make new cap 16 as an unsigned number.
Let old bytes be items's cap times (byte size of T).
Let new bytes be new cap times (byte size of T).
Let resized be resize memory at items's data as a pointer to byte from old bytes to new bytes.
Update items so its data is resized as a pointer to T and its cap is new cap.
End the condition.
Set item items's len of items's data to val.
Add 1 to items's len.

Publicly, to pop for T from items (a pointer to List of T), named list_pop, giving T.
If items's len equals 0, stop with error "pop from empty list".
Set items's len to items's len minus 1.
Give back item items's len of items's data.

Publicly, to get an item for T given items as a pointer to List of T, index as an unsigned number, named list_get, giving T.
If index is at least items's len, stop with error "list index out of bounds".
Give back item index of items's data.

Publicly, to set an item for T given items as a pointer to List of T, index as an unsigned number, val as T, named list_set.
If index is at least items's len, stop with error "list index out of bounds".
Set item index of items's data to val.

Publicly, to count the items for T given items as a pointer to List of T, named list_len, giving an unsigned number.
Call this as "count of <items>".
Give back items's len.

Publicly, to get the first item for T given items as a pointer to List of T, named list_first, giving T.
Call this as "first item of <items>".
If items's len equals 0, stop with error "first item of empty list".
Give back item 0 of items's data.

Publicly, to get the last item for T given items as a pointer to List of T, named list_last, giving T.
Call this as "last item of <items>".
If items's len equals 0, stop with error "last item of empty list".
Give back item (items's len minus 1) of items's data.

Publicly, to clear a list for T given items as a pointer to List of T, named list_clear.
Set items's len to 0 as an unsigned number.

Publicly, to free a list for T given items as a pointer to List of T, named list_free.
Call this as "free list <items>".
If items's data is not empty, release memory at items's data as a pointer to byte.
Update items so its data is no address as a pointer to T, its len is 0 as an unsigned number, and its cap is 0 as an unsigned number.

Publicly, to sort a list for T given items as a pointer to List of T, compare as a function taking pointer to T and pointer to T returning whole number, named list_sort.
Call this as "sort <items> using <compare>".
Let outer be 0 as an unsigned number.
While outer is less than items's len.
Let inner be outer.
While inner is more than 0.
Let left be address of item (inner minus 1) of items's data.
Let right be address of item inner of items's data.
If apply compare to right with left is less than 0, let temporary be item inner of items's data, set item inner of items's data to item (inner minus 1) of items's data, set item (inner minus 1) of items's data to temporary, set inner to inner minus 1, otherwise break.
End the loop.
Add 1 to outer.
End the loop.
